        {"t": "h1", "text": "Day 5 — SQL Server, query performance, and database roles"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Outcome: you can sit with a DBA or a skeptical interviewer and talk about indexes, locking, and the exact permissions the API login has. This is the SQL role conversation: both the query job and the security role."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Time box (3.5 hours)"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "45 min: indexes, SARGability, and a sketch of a plan.",
            "70 min: locking, isolation, deadlocks, parameter sniffing.",
            "40 min: roles and least privilege, including a migration identity versus a runtime identity.",
            "55 min: questions aloud. End by writing GRANT statements you can explain.",
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Concept block"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "A clustered index is the table's row order. One per table. The clustering key rides along in every nonclustered index. A narrow, ever-increasing key such as a bigint identity or a sequential GUID strategy keeps inserts orderly. A random GUID as the clustered key scatters inserts and fragments the leaf.",
            "A nonclustered index is a separate structure on key columns, with the clustering key attached. INCLUDE adds columns at the leaf so a query can be covered and avoid a key lookup.",
            "A seek uses the index to jump to the range. A scan reads all or most of the leaf. A scan is not automatically bad on a small table. A seek is not automatically good if it performs a million key lookups.",
            "SARGable predicates can use an index: column equals value, column greater than value, column in a small list. A function on the column, such as YEAR(CreatedUtc) = 2026, hides the column from the seek. Put the function on the constant, or use a range.",
            "The optimizer uses statistics. A stale statistic after a big load produces a bad plan. Query Store on Azure SQL keeps plans and runtime stats so you can force a known good plan while you fix the query.",
            "Parameter sniffing means SQL caches a plan based on the first parameter values. That plan can be wrong for the next shape. Options include rewriting, a local variable with its own tradeoff, OPTIMIZE FOR, or a targeted recompile. I confirm with the plan and Query Store before I sprinkle hints.",
            "Isolation: READ COMMITTED can block readers and writers on locks. READ COMMITTED SNAPSHOT uses row versions so readers do not block writers for the common case, with statement-level consistency. SNAPSHOT isolation gives transaction-level consistency using versions. Both use the version store. I know which one the database uses before I talk about blocking.",
            "A deadlock is a cycle of locks. SQL picks a victim, error 1205. The application retries if the operation is safe to retry. The lasting fix is a consistent lock order, shorter transactions, and indexes that remove unnecessary scans and extra locks.",
            "Keyset pagination filters on the last key seen. Offset pagination asks SQL to count and skip. Deep offsets get slower as the offset grows.",
            "ACID: atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability. A transaction is the unit. A unique constraint is part of consistency, and I trust it more than a check in C# alone.",
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Database roles — the security model you should draw"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "SQL Server has server roles and database roles. The tech lead answer is least privilege for each identity, not a login in sysadmin or db_owner because the app needs to work."},
        {"t": "table", "headers": ["Identity", "May do", "Must not do"], "rows": [
            ["API runtime, managed identity or SQL user", "SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, EXECUTE on the procedures it calls", "DDL, db_owner, control server, read other databases"],
            ["Function or worker runtime", "Only the tables and procedures that worker uses", "Share the API's broader grants by habit"],
            ["Migration pipeline", "DDL on this database during release", "Be the login the running site uses every day"],
            ["Reporting reader", "SELECT on a schema or a set of views", "INSERT or UPDATE"],
            ["Human admin", "Their own account, audited, privileged through a group", "A shared sa password in a wiki"],
            ["Local developer", "A contained dev database with more rights", "Those rights copied into production"],
        ]},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Built-in database roles you will be asked to name: db_owner can do everything in the database. db_ddladmin can change schema. db_datareader can read all tables. db_datawriter can modify data in all tables. db_denydatawriter blocks writes. These roles are broad. A lead creates a custom role and grants schema-level or object-level permissions."},
        {"t": "code", "text": "CREATE ROLE app_orders_runtime AUTHORIZATION dbo;\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON SCHEMA::orders TO app_orders_runtime;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON SCHEMA::orders TO app_orders_runtime;\nALTER ROLE app_orders_runtime ADD MEMBER [id-api-prod];  -- managed identity user\n\nCREATE ROLE app_orders_migrator AUTHORIZATION dbo;\nGRANT ALTER ON SCHEMA::orders TO app_orders_migrator;\n-- used by the pipeline, not by the App Service"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "On Azure SQL, the application uses a managed identity. You create a user from that identity in the database and add it to the runtime role. No password in the connection string. The connection string uses Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity, or the current Microsoft.Data.SqlClient equivalent your platform standardizes on."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Clustered index, nonclustered index, and key lookup. Explain them with an orders table.",
         "a": "Suppose Orders is clustered on Id, an identity. The rows of the table are stored in Id order. A query by Id is a clustered seek. A query by TenantId and CreatedUtc needs a nonclustered index on (TenantId, CreatedUtc) or it scans. If the select list includes Status and Total, and those columns are not in the index, SQL seeks the nonclustered index and then does a key lookup into the clustered index for every row to fetch Status and Total. A few lookups are fine. Lookups for tens of thousands of rows dominate. I INCLUDE Status and Total to cover the query, or I narrow the select list. Every extra included column slows writes and grows the index, so I cover the hot query, not every column in the table.",
         "lead": "I ask for the actual plan on production-like data, or Query Store's plan, before I add an index. An index that helps this report may slow the insert path the business cares about more. I bring one write-heavy example to the interview so the tradeoff is real."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "A stored procedure that used to be fast is slow for one customer. How do you work the problem?",
         "a": "I capture the statement, the parameters, and the duration from Query Store or from a careful trace. I compare the actual plan to the estimated plan. I look for a scan where a seek should be, a huge mismatch between estimated and actual rows, a key lookup storm, or a spill. I check whether the predicate is SARGable and whether the parameter is atypical, which is parameter sniffing: the plan was compiled for a small tenant and reused for a huge one, or the reverse. I update statistics if a load invalidated them. I fix the query or the index first. I use a hint or a plan guide only when I can name the plan I am pinning and the person who will remove the pin. I also check blocking: a fast query waiting on a lock looks slow in the API and innocent in a local test.",
         "lead": "The API symptom is a latency chart. The SQL symptom is a plan, a wait type, or a lock. I do not scale out the App Service to hide a scan. More instances would run the bad query more often."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Explain isolation, RCSI, and when readers block writers.",
         "a": "Under locking READ COMMITTED, a reader takes shared locks and a writer takes exclusive locks. They block each other. READ COMMITTED SNAPSHOT keeps the read committed semantics for a statement and serves the previous version of a row from the version store, so a reader does not take shared locks that block the writer. Writers still block writers on the same row. SNAPSHOT isolation holds a transaction-level view: the transaction sees the database as of its start. It can fail a write with an update conflict if another transaction changed the row. I enable RCSI when the application was suffering reader-writer blocking and the team accepts row versioning. I check tempdb or the accelerated database recovery version store, because versioning is not free. I still use a concurrency token for lost updates. RCSI does not turn two read-modify-write transactions into a safe increment.",
         "lead": "Before I recommend RCSI in an interview, I say I would confirm the current database setting and the write pattern. Turning it on is a database change with a test plan, not a connection-string flag I flip during an outage without understanding the version store."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What is a deadlock, and what do you change?",
         "a": "Transaction A locks row 1 and wants row 2. Transaction B locks row 2 and wants row 1. SQL detects the cycle and kills one transaction with error 1205. The victim can retry if the work is idempotent and the execution strategy includes 1205. The design fix is to touch resources in the same order in every transaction, to keep transactions short, and to add an index so a query does not lock a wider range than it needs. I read the deadlock graph: the processes, the resources, the statements. I have seen deadlocks caused by an ORM updating every column and taking locks in an order the developer did not intend, and by triggers that lock a second table. Retry without a graph is how the same deadlock stays for a year.",
         "lead": "I put a metric on 1205. A retry policy that succeeds still needs a ticket if the rate climbs. The customer saw a retry. The platform saw a design problem."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you paginate a large table?",
         "a": "Offset pagination, ORDER BY CreatedUtc OFFSET @skip ROWS FETCH NEXT @take ROWS ONLY, is simple and correct for small offsets. For a deep page, SQL reads and discards the skipped rows, and the page shifts if new rows are inserted at the top while the user clicks next. Keyset pagination remembers the last (CreatedUtc, Id) and asks for rows before that pair, with a composite index matching the order. The client sends the cursor, not a page number. I still cap the page size. For an admin UI that must jump to page 400, I talk about the cost openly, or I filter harder so the page number is inside a small set. A covering index makes the page query a seek plus a small range.",
         "lead": "I align the API contract with the SQL. If the product needs stable next-page behavior on a hot table, I do not promise offset page numbers. I design the cursor into the API on day one so we do not break clients later."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Who should the application connect as, and which role should it have?",
         "a": "The running API uses a managed identity in Azure, mapped to a user in Azure SQL, added to a custom role such as app_orders_runtime. That role has data rights on the orders schema and execute rights on the procedures it calls. It is not in db_owner, and it is not db_ddladmin. A separate pipeline identity applies migrations and has the DDL grants. Humans use their own admin accounts through group membership, with auditing. Built-in roles like db_datareader plus db_datawriter are a stepping stone I can explain, and they are broader than I want because they cover every table, including audit and payment tables the API should not touch. I grant at schema level when the schema matches the service boundary. I review a new table's grants as part of the migration PR, because a new table with no grant fails safely, and a new table under db_owner fails open.",
         "lead": "If a panel asks whether db_owner is acceptable because migrations run at startup, I say that is two problems: startup migration and an over-privileged runtime. Split the identity, move DDL to the pipeline, and keep the site on the runtime role. That answer is the SQL role answer they are scoring."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Stored procedure or EF query? Give a decision a team can reuse.",
         "a": "EF is the default for aggregate commands and ordinary filters, because the model, the migration, and the C# rule stay together. A procedure or a hand-written statement is justified when the plan is critical, the SQL is set-based and ugly in LINQ, or a reporting query must be owned and tuned by someone who lives in Query Store. The procedure is parameterized, lives in source control, and is deployed with the migration. Rights are EXECUTE, not a blanket datawriter role, when the procedure is the only path and I want to prevent ad hoc updates. I do not move every CRUD action into procedures. That splits the team in half and hides business rules from the people who change them most often.",
         "lead": "I write the decision down. Changing it per developer is how one service ends up with three data-access styles and no owner for performance."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What is a unique constraint doing that application code cannot?",
         "a": "Two API instances can both check that an email is free and both insert. The check in C# is not atomic across processes. A unique index is. I use unique constraints for business identity: one idempotency key, one external reference per tenant, one active subscription. I catch the unique violation and return 409. I still validate early for a friendly message, and I accept that the database is the authority under concurrency. A filtered unique index can enforce uniqueness only for active rows, which is the right tool when soft-deleted rows should not block a new value.",
         "lead": "I look for check-then-act in code review. If the bug would create two charges or two users, the fix is a constraint, not a deeper if statement."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Waits and words worth knowing"},
